# PHASE 2 BUILD PROMPT \'97 SCAFFOLD & BUILD `LoopStep` (ROLL NO: `24b3007`) We have committed `DESIGN.md` in `et617-endsem-24b3007`[cite: 1, 9]. Now build the working educational application in clean, verifiable increments so we hit our **1:00 first live Vercel deployment** (`et617-24b3007.vercel.app`)[cite: 2, 8, 9]. Every single component, screen, AI call, and UI interaction you build MUST strictly obey the verbatim exam card specification below (a mismatch on any field scores zero)[cite: 1]: --- ## 0. MANDATORY EXAM CARD SPECIFICATION (SUPREME SOURCE OF TRUTH) * **Identity Token:** `24b3007` (must appear in the **footer of every single screen** and on a dedicated **About screen**)[cite: 2, 8]. * **Repository & Live URL:** `et617-endsem-24b3007` deployed at `https://et617-24b3007.vercel.app`[cite: 2, 8, 9]. * **Problem Statement (`P4 \'97 Adaptive tutor with automatic question generation` | *Adaptive learning \'b7 Assessment \'b7 Generative AI*):**[cite: 3] > "Design a tutor for your card's topic that generates questions based on the learner's performance, misconceptions and previous responses. It must go beyond multiple-choice: support at least three question formats, evaluate learner explanations, diagnose misconceptions and adjust the difficulty or nature of the next task."[cite: 4] * **Mandatory Design Challenge (`P4`):** > "How does the tutor decide whether the learner needs a harder question, an alternative explanation or prerequisite instruction? State the decision rule and show each of the three outcomes."[cite: 4] * **Topic (`P4-T10`):** > "Loops in block or Python programming (Cl. 8\'9611)"[cite: 6] * **Learner Context (`L5`):** > "Learner repeating the topic after failing it once, high anxiety and low self-efficacy"[cite: 7] * **Design Constraint (`C4 \'97 10-minute sessions`):** > "One complete learning sequence fits in 10 minutes; the learner can stop and resume where they left off" (must be visibly implemented in the working app)[cite: 7, 8]. --- ## STEP 1: UI/UX RESEARCH & ANTI-GENERIC DESIGN SYSTEM (DO THIS FIRST) **CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENT:** Do **NOT** generate a generic AI template (no purple gradients, no SaaS marketing hero sections, no dark hacker-terminal clich\'e9s, no cluttered chat sidebars). Design specifically for **Aarav (`L5`: Class 10 student repeating Python loops after failing once, with high anxiety and low self-efficacy)**[cite: 7, 8]. Before writing UI components, use your web search / browser tools to inspect the UI/UX patterns and interactive affordances of: 1. **Python Tutor (`pythontutor.com`)** \'97 how it visually highlights the active execution line, next-line arrow, and step-by-step variable state frames so learners do not have to hold state in working memory. 2. **Runestone Academy / Interactive Parsons Problems** \'97 how block-ordering tasks separate unsorted code blocks from the indented solution workspace with clear indentation guides. 3. **Brilliant.org / Khan Academy** \'97 how single-concept micro-steps, generous whitespace, warm paper/stone surfaces, and gentle inline feedback lower cognitive overload and test anxiety[cite: 11, 12]. Synthesize these into a **Warm Tactile Notebook & Live Code-Bench aesthetic**: * **Color Palette:** Warm off-white paper canvas (`#FAF8F5`), crisp elevated cards (`#FFFFFF`) with subtle stone borders (`#E7E5E4`), deep charcoal ink (`#1C1917`), calm sage green (`#15803D` / `#DCFCE7`) for validated steps, warm amber (`#B45309` / `#FEF3C7`) for gentle misconception diagnostics (never harsh red error banners!), and indigo-slate (`#3730A3`) for active code line highlighting[cite: 11, 12]. * **Layout & Voice:** Clean sans-serif UI paired with monospace (`JetBrains Mono` / `Fira Code`) for Python code and variable trace cells. Single-column focused workspace (`max-w-3xl`) using warm, conversational first-person plural language ("we", "let's") that normalizes mistakes[cite: 11, 12]. --- ## STEP 2: INITIALIZE PROJECT & MAINTAIN `README.md` CONTINUOUSLY 1. Scaffold a **Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS** app inside `et617-endsem-24b3007` alongside `DESIGN.md`, installing `@google/genai`, `zod`, and `lucide-react`[cite: 2, 9]. 2. Create and **continuously update `README.md`** after every milestone so it serves as our live engineering & Viva log, documenting: * Exam Card metadata (`P4-T10-L5-C4`, Roll No: `24b3007`, Live URL: `https://et617-24b3007.vercel.app`)[cite: 2, 8]. * UI/UX Inspirations & Pedagogical Rationale (what was adapted from Python Tutor, Parsons Problems, and Brilliant.org, and how `L5` + `C4` shaped the interface)[cite: 9]. * Architecture & File Map (mapping every file to deliverables `D1`\'96`D8`)[cite: 8, 9]. * Explicit **3-Way Decision Rule** table (`Harder Question` vs. `Alternative Explanation` vs. `Prerequisite Instruction`)[cite: 4]. * **Changelog / Build Log** (updated each time a module or feature is completed to support our 30-minute incremental commits and report)[cite: 1, 9]. --- ## STEP 3: BUILD CORE ENGINE MODULES (`src/lib/`) Implement these three pure-TypeScript modules with complete, real Python loop content (zero placeholder text)[cite: 8]: 1. **`src/lib/loopTracer.ts` (Topic-Specific Problem-Solving Tool \'97 Rubric T6):**[cite: 13, 14] * Deterministic step-by-step execution tracer for Python `for` loops (`range(stop)`, `range(start, stop)`, `range(start, stop, step)`) with an accumulator variable (`total`) and optional `if` filter. * Produces execution frames: `\{ stepIndex, lineNumber, codeLine, loopVarName, loopVarValue, accumulatorName, accumulatorValue, printedOutput, explanationNote \}`. * Includes `findDivergenceStep(learnerTrace, actualTrace)` to highlight the exact iteration where a learner's predicted values first diverged from Python's actual state (e.g., Pass 4 when `i=4` is wrongly included in `range(1, 4)`). 2. **`src/lib/curriculum.ts` (D3 Content + D6 Deterministic Fallback Bank):**[cite: 8, 10] * Full expanded `L5` persona (`Aarav Kulkarni`, Cla